WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.668
[bees buzzing]

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:03.169
NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON:
For thousands of years,

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:06.539
bees have been symbols
of mindless industry.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:08.375
We always think of
them as being something

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:12.078
like biological robots,
doomed to live out their lives

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.981
in lockstep, shackled
to the dreary roles

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.817
assigned to them by nature.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.920
This is our first contact story.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:25.024
It happened in a place called
Brunnwinkl in rural Austria

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.526
in the early 1900s.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:29.596
[music playing]

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:34.467
From the time Karl von
Frisch was a child,

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.971
he longed to understand
what the other animals knew,

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.206
how they perceived the world.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.242
He wanted to know
if tiny fish saw

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:45.211
color or had a sense of smell.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.314
He invented experiments to
explore animal experience.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.583
And he filmed them.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.752
Starting in the
early 20th century,

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:56.055
he was the first to use the
new medium of motion pictures

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.224
to create popular
science entertainment

00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:00.960
and communication.

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:04.831
For thousands of years, humans
have noted the eccentric dances

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.900
of the bees.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.168
But no one had
ever looked at them

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.270
with the kind of respect
that assumed there

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:13.206
was a reason to their dancing.

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:15.542
Before Karl von
Frisch, no one ever

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:18.645
thought to ask why they
moved this way and that way,

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:22.282
in a succession of
elaborate figure eights.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:25.452
Von Frisch studied
every tiny bee gesture

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:30.123
and became fascinated by a
mystery he couldn't explain.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.025
He would set out a
dish of sugar water

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:35.595
for a bee from his
experimental hive.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:38.631
The bee would feast upon
it before flying back home.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.471
The marked bee would
later return to dine

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.706
on the delicious sugar water.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.642
Von Frisch noted that
in just a few hours,

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:54.514
a multitude of other bees
would join her there.

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:56.749
They were always her
fellow hive mates.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.421
But here was the
really amazing thing.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:03.523
Von Frisch knew
that the other bees

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.926
had not followed the marked
bee to the feeding place.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.394
How?

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.364
Because he had the hive
closely watched at all times.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.267
He had been careful to use
sugar water, not honey,

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.336
so that the bees'
sense of smell could

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:18.638
not guide them to the reward.

00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:21.107
He continued to move
the dish of sugar water

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.977
farther away until it
was several kilometers

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.411
from the hive.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.948
Still, the hive mates
would find their way to it.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:32.218
So how did the painted bee
reveal the exact location

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.287
of the sugar water
with such precision

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:36.322
that her hive mates
could unerringly

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:37.924
find their way there?

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.426
[music playing]

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.564
There was a secret message
in her choreography.

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:47.800
What had seemed to countless
generations of observers

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.371
to be nothing more than the
meaningless spasmodic motions

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:55.575
of a dumb animal was
actually a complex message,

00:02:55.642 --> 00:03:00.013
an equation informed by
mathematics, astronomy,

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.082
and an acute
knowledge of time, all

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.451
synthesized to
convey the location

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.154
of the riches she hoped
to share with her sisters.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:15.895
The dancer use the angle
of our star, the sun,

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.797
to indicate the
general direction

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:18.631
of the food's location.

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:26.105
Von Frisch noted that when a
bee danced straight upward,

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.508
she meant "fly toward the sun."

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:32.612
And when she moved downward,
she meant "fly away from it."

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.114
Her swivels, left
and right, conveyed

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.817
the food's exact
coordinates in space,

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.752
sometimes kilometers away.

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:51.097
The duration of her dance,
down to a fraction of a second,

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:53.032
indicated the length
of time it would take

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.801
her fellow bees to get there.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:04:01.774
She even factored in wind
speed to more finely calibrate

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:02.742
the message she danced.

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:08.781
And this was true at
any time of the year

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:12.485
and from hive to hive, from
continent to continent.

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:16.289
Bees can do the math.

